"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including a preoperative PSA of 8.6 and an ISUP grade group of 3 from the prostatectomy specimen. Contributing to risk are positive surgical margins and evidence of extraprostatic extension (pT3a). Countervailing findings include no documented seminal vesicle invasion and no lymph node metastasis reported in the surgical pathology. The biopsy-to-prostatectomy comparison shows concordance regarding Gleason pattern, with both identifying Grade 4+3. Missing postoperative PSA data significantly widens prognostic uncertainty, as its subsequent trajectory is critical for risk stratification. Further information regarding the postoperative PSA level and its rate of change would substantially revise this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 51.41 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 38.56–64.26 months; its coverage has not been validated."
